package "Fitness Company Conan" {

    class Center {
        - name: String
        - address: String
        - rooms: Room*
    }

    class Room {
        - number: int
        - maximumCapacity: int
    }

    class Person {
        - firstName: String
        - familyName: String
        - birthDate: Date
    }

    class Trainer {
        - diploma: String
    }

    class Session {
        - date: Date
        - startingHour: Time
    }

    class IndividualSession {
        * participatesIn: Session
    }

    class GroupSession {
        - type: String
        * hasTrainer: Trainer
    }

    Center "1" -- "*" Room
    Room "1" -- "*" Session
    Session "1" -- "*" IndividualSession
    Session "1" -- "*" GroupSession
    GroupSession "1" -- "*" Trainer

    note right of Person : Combination of firstName, familyName, and birthDate is unique
    note right of Trainer : Inherits from Person
    note right of GroupSession : Exactly one Trainer (aggregation)
    note right of Room : Room number is unique per Center
    note right of Session : Date and startingHour are unique per Room/Center combination
}

@enduml
```

This diagram includes:
1. A `Center` class with unique name and address, containing multiple `Room` objects
2. `Room` class with unique number and capacity
3. `Person` base class with name and birth date
4. `Trainer` subclass with diploma field
5. `Session` base class with date and time
6. Two session types (`IndividualSession` and `GroupSession`) with appropriate relationships
7. Notes specifying important constraints from the problem statement

The relationships show:
- Center contains Rooms (composition)
- Rooms contain Sessions (composition)
- Sessions can be either individual or group (inheritance)
- Group sessions require exactly one trainer (aggregation)
- Prospects and interns are tracked through the base `Person` class<｜end▁of▁sentence｜>

